{"word": "hydrogen", "source": "freedictionaryapi", "data": {"word": "hydrogen", "entries": [{"language": {"code": "en", "name": "English"}, "partOfSpeech": "noun", "pronunciations": [{"type": "ipa", "text": "/ˈhaɪdɹəd͡ʒ(ə)n/", "tags": ["Received Pronunciation"]}, {"type": "ipa", "text": "/ˈhaɪdɹəd͡ʒ(ə)n/", "tags": ["General American"]}, {"type": "ipa", "text": "[ˈhaɪd͡ʒɹəd͡ʒən]", "tags": ["sometimes", "General American"]}], "forms": [{"word": "hydrogens", "tags": ["plural"]}], "senses": [{"definition": "(uncountable) The lightest chemical element (symbol H), with an atomic number of 1 and atomic weight of 1.008.", "tags": ["uncountable"], "examples": ["Our professor then reminded us that a hydrogen ion is merely a proton, and thus we should think about acid-base theory as we consider this problem further."], "quotes": [{"text": "The ignis fatuus or Jack a lantern, ſo frequently alluded to by poets, is ſuppoſed to originate from the inflammable air, or Hydrogene, given up from moraſſes; […]", "reference": "1791, [Erasmus Darwin], “Canto I”, in The Botanic Garden; a Poem, in Two Parts. […], London: J[oseph] Johnson, […], →OCLC, part I (The Economy of Vegetation), footnote, page 18:"}, {"text": "But my Leech—a leech / Fit to suck blood, with lubricous round rings, / Capaciously expatiative, which make / His little body like a red balloon, / As full of blood as that of hydrogen, / Sucked from men's hearts; […]", "reference": "1820, Sophocles, “Œdipus Tyrannus; or, Swellfoot the Tyrant. A Tragedy, in Two Acts. […]”, in Percy Bysshe Shelley, transl., edited by [Mary] Shelley, The Poetical Works of Percy Bysshe Shelley. […], new edition, London: Edward Moxon […], published 1840, →OCLC, Act I, page 184:"}, {"text": "Water is the sole product of the combustion of hydrogen gas. For this important fact we are indebted to Mr. [Henry] Cavendish. He demonstrated it by burning oxygen and hydrogen gases in a dry glass vessel, when a quantity of pure water was generated exactly equal in weight to that of the gases which had disappeared.", "reference": "1827, Edward Turner, “Section IV. Hydrogen.”, in Elements of Chemistry: Including the Recent Discoveries and Doctrines of the Science, Edinburgh: […] William Tait, […]; and Charles Tait, […], part II (Inorganic Chemistry), page 160:"}, {"text": "It was an enormous balloon filled with helium, that elemental gas forged from hydrogen in the nuclear furnace of the stars, first step along the way in the generation of multiplicity and variety of matter in the universe, including our selves and all our thoughts.", "reference": "1997, Ian McEwan, chapter 1, in Enduring Love, [United Kingdom]: The Softback Preview, →OCLC, page 3:"}], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": [], "translations": [{"language": {"code": "ab", "name": "Abkhaz"}, "word": "аӡри"}, {"language": {"code": "af", "name": "Afrikaans"}, "word": "waterstof"}, {"language": {"code": "sq", "name": "Albanian"}, "word": "hidrogjen"}, {"language": {"code": "am", "name": "Amharic"}, "word": "ሃይድሮጅን"}, {"language": {"code": "ar", "name": "Arabic"}, "word": "هَيْدُرُوجِين"}, {"language": {"code": "ar", "name": "Arabic"}, "word": "هِيدْرُوجِين"}, {"language": {"code": "an", "name": "Aragonese"}, "word": "hidrocheno"}, {"language": {"code": "hy", "name": "Armenian"}, "word": "ջրածին"}, {"language": {"code": "ast", "name": "Asturian"}, "word": "hidróxenu"}, {"language": {"code": "az", "name": "Azerbaijani"}, "word": "مولدالماء"}, {"language": {"code": "az", "name": "Azerbaijani"}, "word": "hidrogen"}, {"language": {"code": "az", "name": "Azerbaijani"}, "word": "müvəllidülma"}, {"language": {"code": "eu", "name": "Basque"}, "word": "hidrogeno"}, {"language": {"code": "be", "name": "Belarusian"}, "word": "вадаро́д"}, {"language": {"code": "be", "name": "Belarusian"}, "word": "воднік"}, {"language": {"code": "bn", "name": "Bengali"}, "word": "উদজান"}, {"language": {"code": "bn", "name": "Bengali"}, "word": "হাইড্রোজেন"}, {"language": {"code": "bho", "name": "Bho